1
00:05:53,333 --> 00:05:59,333
Another world… as close as the beat of your heart

2
00:11:46,666 --> 00:11:52,666
Photographing Fairies is a mystery, fantasy,
drama film directed by Nick Willing.

3
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:46,000
Photographer Charles Castle is numbed with grief
following the death of his beautiful bride.

4
00:23:33,333 --> 00:23:39,333
He goes off to war, working in the trenches as a photographer.

5
00:29:26,666 --> 00:29:32,666
Following the war and still in grief Charles is given some
photographs purporting to be of fairies.

6
00:35:20,000 --> 00:35:26,000
His search for the truth leads him to Burkinwell, a
seemingly peaceful village seething with secrets

7
00:41:13,333 --> 00:41:19,333
Charles Castle, played by Toby Stephens.

8
00:47:06,666 --> 00:47:12,666
Linda, played by Emily Woof.

9
00:53:00,000 --> 00:53:06,000
Rev. Templeton, played by Ben Kingsley.

10
00:58:53,333 --> 00:58:59,333
Beatrice Templeton, played by Frances Barber.

11
01:04:46,666 --> 01:04:52,666
Doctor, played by Bernard Gallagher.

12
01:10:40,000 --> 01:10:46,000
Roy, played by Phil Davis.

13
01:16:33,333 --> 01:16:39,333
Photographing Fairies is a 1997 British fantasy film based on
Steve Szilagyi's 1992 novel Photographing Fairies.

14
01:22:26,666 --> 01:22:32,666
The film explores some of the themes of folklore, such as possession,
paganism, animism, hallucinogens, parapsychology and fairies.

15
01:28:20,000 --> 01:28:26,000
It was inspired by the Cottingley Fairies hoax, and was
released in the United Kingdom on 19 September 1997.

16
01:34:13,333 --> 01:34:19,333
The film explores themes of based on novel or book,
photographer, loss of loved one, fairy, grieving widower.

17
01:40:06,666 --> 01:40:12,666
The tone is emotional and mind_bending.
